+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best open source AI tools for lawyers in 2026?
+For legal-specific work, the strongest GitHub repos in this list are OpenContracts, Opennyai, LexNLP, LawGlance, and PAKTON. For the surrounding document pipeline, Docling, Marker, OCRmyPDF, Presidio, and Haystack are often more production-critical than a chat UI alone.
+
+### Are there fully self-hosted AI tools for lawyers on GitHub?
+Yes. OpenContracts, Onyx, AnythingLLM, DocuSeal, Documenso, OpenSign, Haystack, and many OCR/document parsing repos can be self-hosted. Review each project license carefully before firm-wide deployment.
+
+### What open source tools help lawyers analyze contracts?
+OpenContracts, LexNLP, ContextGem, PAKTON, Marker, and Docling are the most relevant repos in this list for contract review, extraction, clause analysis, and long-agreement question answering.
+
+### What open source AI tools help lawyers search scanned PDFs and exhibits?
+OCRmyPDF, Tesseract, PaddleOCR, Surya, Layout Parser, Marker, and Docling are the core open source GitHub options for making scanned legal documents searchable and AI-ready.
+
+### Which GitHub repos are best for redaction and privacy in legal AI workflows?
+Presidio is the strongest general option here. scrubadub and its extensions are smaller but useful. This is still a real open source gap compared with OCR and RAG tooling.
+
+### Are there enough true open source legal AI repos yet?
+Not really. There are enough repos to build a strong stack, but legal-specific open source remains much thinner than adjacent categories like OCR, document conversion, and RAG orchestration. That is why some categories here rely on legal-adjacent infrastructure rather than lawyer-only products.
+
+### What should I avoid when choosing open source AI tools for lawyers?
+Avoid repos without clear licenses, repos that depend on closed hosted services for core functionality, and repos that cannot export structured outputs. Also avoid assuming a generic chat app is sufficient if your real bottleneck is OCR, parsing, or redaction quality.
+
