+## TL;DR
+- **Best legal-specific workspace:** [OpenContracts](https://github.com/JSv4/OpenContracts) for document analytics, extraction, redaction, and legal document workflows.
+- **Best legal NLP pipeline:** [Opennyai](https://github.com/OpenNyAI/Opennyai) if your work includes court judgments, structured legal text, and explainable NLP components.
+- **Best document conversion stack:** [Docling](https://github.com/docling-project/docling), [Marker](https://github.com/datalab-to/marker), and [OCRmyPDF](https://github.com/ocrmypdf/OCRmyPDF) for turning messy PDFs into searchable, LLM-ready inputs.
+- **Best self-hosted knowledge assistants:** [Onyx](https://github.com/onyx-dot-app/onyx), [AnythingLLM](https://github.com/Mintplex-Labs/anything-llm), and [Haystack](https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack) for internal matter knowledge bases and firm-wide search.
+- **Best privacy stack:** [Presidio](https://github.com/microsoft/presidio) plus [scrubadub](https://github.com/LeapBeyond/scrubadub) for redaction, de-identification, and safer handling of client-sensitive material.
+
+## Why This List
+Open source AI tools matter for lawyers because legal work is document-heavy, confidentiality-sensitive, and increasingly shaped by retrieval, extraction, summarization, and workflow automation. The best AI tools for lawyers are not just flashy demos: they need inspectable code, self-hosting paths, exportable data, and licenses your team can evaluate before moving sensitive matters into production.
+
+This list deliberately excludes proprietary SaaS products and only includes tools that exist as public GitHub repositories. Where a category is still thin, that gap is called out directly instead of padded with unrelated products.
