+# Contributing
+
+Thanks for contributing to `awesome-best-ai-tools-for-lawyers-2026`.
+
+## Submission Rules
+
+- Every submission must include a public `github.com` repository URL.
+- No proprietary SaaS products.
+- No closed-source wrappers around closed APIs unless the GitHub repository itself provides meaningful open source functionality.
+- The repository must have a visible license on GitHub.
+- The tool must be relevant to lawyers, legal ops, contract review, legal research, OCR, redaction, legal NLP, annotation, or self-hosted legal document workflows.
+
+## Required Submission Format
+
+Please open a pull request with:
+
+1. Repository name
+2. GitHub URL
+3. One-paragraph description
+4. Stars count
+5. Primary language
+6. License
+7. Last visible GitHub activity date
+8. Best legal use case
+
+Example:
+
+```md
+#### [Tool Name](https://github.com/owner/repo)
+> **Description:** Short summary from the repository README plus why it matters for lawyers.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/owner/repo](https://github.com/owner/repo)
+- **Stars:** 1234 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-01-15
+- **Category:** contracts, extraction
+- **Best for:** clause extraction from agreements
+```
+
+## Quality Standard
+
+- Prefer verified, active repositories over abandoned demos.
+- If a category is thin, note the gap instead of stretching relevance.
+- Keep descriptions factual and derived from the repository README and GitHub metadata.
+- Do not remove an existing tool without explaining why in the pull request.
+
+## Local Checks
+
+Before submitting:
+
+```bash
+markdownlint README.md CONTRIBUTING.md
+```
+
+If `markdownlint` is unavailable, at minimum confirm the Markdown renders correctly on GitHub.
+
+## License
+
+By contributing, you agree that your contributions will be licensed under the MIT License in this repository.
